  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic comparison of how particles move through a porous or network-like medium under an applied downward driving force. In the top row, all particles are shown as filled yellow circles and the blue arrows point downward, indicating uniform drift with the external force. In the second row, some particles are replaced by open circles and one red arrow points upward, suggesting a second component or vacancy-like state that can move against the main driving direction.\n\nThe lower panels place these particles on an ordered lattice of intersecting channels. Under the downward bias, most motion follows the slanted links and vertical direction. A dashed red circle labeled \\(R_b\\) highlights a local region where transport or rearrangement is being considered. In the left lattice, the filled particles occupy the network more uniformly. In the right lattice, several open circles appear within the highlighted region and along one branch, accompanied by red upward arrows, showing a localized counterflow or backflow process within the otherwise downward-moving system.\n\nOverall, the figure illustrates that introducing a second state or defect changes the local transport pattern, creating upward motion in a confined region even though the global drive remains downward.",
